### The corp ladder: Respawn (gamers), Rockstarved, and the Chicago cell.
### Parody brands. Every grievance is one real gamers already have; every
### crime is invented and pinned only on the fictional company.

=== pixel
-- start
? q.mq_ms.done -> ms_after
? q.mq_ms>=50 -> ms_win
? flag.chi_home -> home
? q.mq_chi1.started -> chi
? flag.rs_done -> rs_after
? flag.joined_respawn -> member
? flag.pixel_met -> met
> The arcade's back terminal lights up with a video call. A mask on the other end, a cartoon pixel-heart with an arrow through it, voice run through the same cheap filter Mr. Robot uses.
PIXEL: fsociety. Finally. I've been screaming into E Corp's feedback form for three years. You know what a feedback form is? It's a trash can with a progress bar.
PIXEL: I run Respawn. Ten thousand of us. People who bought games E Corp switched off. Accounts E Corp locked for a refund request. Studios E Corp bought and turned into a logo on a launcher. We're not asking for the world back. We're asking for the games we paid for.
* "Why come to us?" -> why
* "Games. There are bigger fights." -> bigger
* "I'm listening. Who's first?" -> first
-- why
PIXEL: Because you did Five Nine— no. Sorry. Because you're the only crew that ever actually pressed the button instead of writing a thread about pressing the button. We write threads. We're very good at threads. We need someone who does things.
-> first
-- bigger
PIXEL: Bigger. Sure. The debt, the banks, the whole machine. And who do you think OWNS the machine's favorite toys? E Corp bought every studio worth loving and bled it. Gaming's not a side quest, friend. It's the beta test for owning everything. They tried the loot box on kids first because kids don't have lawyers.
! trust robot 0
-> first
-- first
PIXEL: Start small. Start cruel. Rockstarved Games. One masterpiece a decade, and in between they sell pretend money for real money and call it a 'live service.' A kid in my Discord spent his late mom's savings on their Shark Cards. Fourteen. They sent him a thank-you email with a confetti gif.
PIXEL: Their studio's on Floor 88 of the North Tower. E Corp owns the building through four shell companies and a sense of humor. Burn them down — not the building, the business model — and nine thousand people watch the little guy win for once. Then the next nine corps get scared.
* "I'm in. Respawn's got a hacker now." -> join
* "I'll think about it." -> think
-- join
PIXEL: PRESS START, baby. Okay. Okay okay okay. I'm putting the word out. Rockstarved. Floor 88, North Tower. Make it hurt where they keep their quarterly earnings.
! set joined_respawn ; set pixel_met ; quest mq_respawn 20 ; quest mq_rs1 10 ; trust robot 1 ; fame gamers 5 ; xp 60
-> END
-- think
PIXEL: Think. We've been thinking for three console generations. Door's open. The forum's always open. That's the whole problem with the forum.
! set pixel_met ; quest mq_respawn 20
-> END
-- met
PIXEL: Rockstarved. Floor 88, North Tower. You'll need a way up past the lobby — try the ex-QA guys who drink their feelings at The Rabbit Hole. They kept their badges. Nobody at Rockstarved ever turns anything off. That's sort of the point.
* "On it." -> go
* "Tell me about Respawn." -> about
-- about
PIXEL: Streamers who got demonetized for telling the truth. Modders E Corp sued for fixing their games for free. QA testers crunched for ninety hours and laid off by an automated email the day after launch. Kids with refund tickets that'll never be read. That's Respawn. We're the people the industry calls 'entitled' for wanting to own what we bought.
-> go
-- go
PIXEL: Go make them regret the confetti gif.
! quest mq_respawn 20 ; quest mq_rs1 10
-> END
-- member
? flag.rs_done -> rs_after
PIXEL: How's Rockstarved coming? Floor 88. Project Whale. The thing's a spreadsheet that decides which of us is worth lying to.
-> END
-- rs_after
? q.mq_chi1.started -> chi
PIXEL: You actually did it. Rockstarved's stock did the fun kind of line. The angry kind. Three more studios 'paused' their live-service updates 'to listen to the community.' They never listen. They're scared. You made them scared.
PIXEL: There's more. Electronic Arse. Phony. They run their whole racket out of Chicago. Darlene's got a cell out there in a dead warehouse. Seven hundred and ninety miles. Hope you like that plane.
! quest mq_rs2 20 ; quest mq_rs2 done ; quest mq_respawn done ; quest mq_chi1 10 ; fame gamers 4 ; xp 40
-> END
-- chi
? flag.phony_freed -> chi_done
? flag.earse_exposed -> chi_half
PIXEL: Chicago. The South Side warehouse. Electronic Arse and Phony are both out there. Go west, friend. Drive it or fly it, but go.
* "How do I get there?" -> chi_how
* "Who's Ansel?" -> chi_ansel
* "On my way." -> END
-- chi_how
PIXEL: West side of the city, there's an on-ramp with a big green sign. I-80. Follow it till the trees get boring, then keep going till they get interesting again. There's a diner on the way with pie that Respawn has a forty-page thread about. Or steal a plane. I'm not your dad.
-> END
-- chi_ansel
PIXEL: Runs the Chicago cell. Hardware genius, soldered her first mod chip at twelve. She's also the only one of us who's met a real E Corp lawyer in person. She says they don't blink. I think she's exaggerating. I hope she's exaggerating.
-> END
-- chi_half
PIXEL: The loot-box odds are on every storefront in the world right now and the forum crashed. Twice. We crashed our own forum celebrating. Phony's next, same floor. Finish it.
-> END
-- chi_done
PIXEL: Every revoked library, unlocked. My inbox is just people posting screenshots of games they thought were gone forever. Somebody got back the game their dad bought them the year before he died. That's it. That's the whole reason. Come home, friend.
-> END
-- home
? flag.pixel_home -> home_again
> The video call connects. For the first time, there's no mask. A tired guy in his thirties with a cartoon heart tattooed on his wrist, in a bedroom full of old cartridges.
PIXEL: Figured you'd earned the face. I'm Dev. I fix arcade cabinets in Yonkers. That's all I ever wanted to do, and the company that bought the last cabinet factory shut it down to sell a subscription.
PIXEL: Three corps in two cities. Rockstarved, Electronic Arse, Phony. Every one of them said the same thing in their apology posts: 'we hear you.' They didn't hear us for ten years. They heard YOU. In a week.
* "It wasn't me. It was ten thousand of you." -> home_crowd
* "Microslop's next." -> home_next
* "Was it worth it?" -> home_worth
-- home_crowd
PIXEL: ...Yeah. Yeah, okay. I'm going to screenshot that and put it on the forum banner. Don't argue. You can't argue with a banner.
! set pixel_home ; fame gamers 4 ; stab 2 ; xp 50
-> ms_plan
-- home_next
PIXEL: Microslop. The big one. Buys a studio a quarter and turns it into a tile on a subscription menu. You read my mind. Or Darlene told you. Probably Darlene.
! set pixel_home ; set microslop_rattled ; fame gamers 2 ; xp 50
-> ms_plan
-- home_worth
PIXEL: My kid sister played her first game this weekend. Bought it, downloaded it, owns it. Nobody can take it back. She doesn't know there was ever a world where they could. ...So, yeah. It was worth it.
! set pixel_home ; stab 3 ; xp 50
-> ms_plan
-- ms_plan
PIXEL: Okay. One more. The one Respawn's been too scared to say out loud. Microslop.
PIXEL: Friday morning, Floor 101 of the North Tower, they unveil 'Game Pass Away.' Every game from every studio they ever bought, moved into one subscription, and then, at nine AM, every copy anybody ever PAID for gets converted into a rental. Stop paying, your library goes dark. Forever.
PIXEL: And the studios? Closed. All of them. They trained an AI on everything those people ever made and called it SlopForge. It's going to 'make' the sequels. They're doing it live on stage. As a bit.
* "How do we get in?" -> ms_how
* "Why is this the one?" -> ms_why
-- ms_why
PIXEL: Because the others sold you a bad game. Microslop's selling the idea that you'll never own anything again, and that the people who make the stuff you love are a cost to be optimized out. If that works on games, it works on everything. Books. Music. Your car's heated seats. It's the beta test, remember? This is the launch.
-> ms_how
-- ms_how
PIXEL: Here's the funny part. They invited kennyQA. As a 'community voice.' They want the guy who took down Rockstarved standing in the front row clapping, so it looks like we approve. Kenny's at The Rabbit Hole, drinking about it. Get his invitation. Get on that floor. Find the Library: the license server behind the stage.
PIXEL: What you do when you're in there is up to you. You've earned that. Just... make it the kind of thing they put in the title.
! quest mq_ms 20 ; xp 40
-> END
-- home_again
? q.mq_ms>=40 -> nag_floor
? q.mq_ms>=30 -> nag_lobby
? q.mq_ms>=20 -> nag_kenny
PIXEL: Respawn's never been bigger. Thirty thousand now. Half of them want to be you and the other half want to be Kenny. Kenny has a fan club. Kenny is not handling it well.
-> END
-- nag_kenny
PIXEL: Kenny. The Rabbit Hole. The invitation. He keeps texting me pictures of it on fire and then saying 'jk.' Get it before he's not joking.
-> END
-- nag_lobby
PIXEL: You've got the invite? The far-right elevator in the WTC lobby. Floor 101. Try to look enthusiastic. Practice in a mirror. No, not like that. Worse.
-> END
-- nag_floor
PIXEL: You're on the floor? Behind the stage. The Library. Whatever you're going to do, do it before Brad Pitchley says 'one more thing.'
-> END
-- ms_win
? flag.ms_freed -> win_freed
? flag.ms_returned -> win_returned
PIXEL: The deck is everywhere. Every studio Microslop owns read it at the same time, and you know what developers do when they all find out at once? They talk to each other. Three acquisitions frozen. A class action with SlopForge's name on it. Brad Pitchley's out. The keynote never happened.
-> win_end
-- win_freed
PIXEL: Two billion libraries. OWNED. No account, no internet, no 'until we change it.' My mom just texted me a photo of her playing the card game that came free with her first computer. She's never texted me a photo of anything. Microslop's stock halted. Twice.
-> win_end
-- win_returned
PIXEL: Forty studios. Their names, their games, their code, back in the hands of the people who made them. One of them already posted a job listing. For a QA tester. It says 'no crunch.' It says it in bold. Kenny applied in eleven seconds.
-> win_end
-- win_end
PIXEL: They'll come back. They always do. But they'll come back knowing that ten thousand people and one very strange hacker can find the switch. ...Thank you, friend. Respawn's yours, whenever you want it.
* "It was always yours." -> win_yours
* "Go fix some arcade cabinets, Dev." -> win_dev
-- win_yours
PIXEL: Then it's ours. Okay. Okay okay okay. GG.
! quest mq_ms done ; achieve microslop ; fame gamers 5 ; stab 3 ; xp 150
-> END
-- win_dev
PIXEL: Ha. Yeah. There's a Galaga in Yonkers with a dead joystick that's been waiting for me since before all this. First thing tomorrow. GG, friend.
! quest mq_ms done ; achieve microslop ; fame gamers 5 ; stab 3 ; xp 150
-> END
-- ms_after
PIXEL: Respawn's thread about you is four thousand pages now. Half of it is people arguing about whether you're real. I told them you're a very committed bit. Go outside. Touch grass. That's an order from your guild leader.
-> END

=== kenny
-- start
? flag.kenny_badge -> after
? !q.mq_rs1.started -> early
> A thin guy at the end of the bar with a lanyard he never took off: ROCKSTARVED GAMES — QA — CRUNCH TEAM C. He's talking to his drink about frame pacing.
KENNY: You don't look like QA. QA has a specific kind of dead in the eyes. Took me three years to earn mine.
* "You worked at Rockstarved." -> worked
* "Rough shift?" -> shift
* [SNEAK 30] (Let him keep talking while you reach for the lanyard.) -> lift | lift_fail
-- early
KENNY: Whatever you're selling, I spent my severance on rent. Which was one week's severance, for three years. Math.
-> END
-- worked
KENNY: Worked. Past tense. Ninety-hour weeks for the launch. Day after it shipped and reviewed a ninety-four? Automated email. 'Your position has been eliminated.' No name on it. A bot fired me from the game I bled for.
KENNY: And you know what they kept running the whole time? Shark Cards. The pretend-money store. That never crunches. That never gets laid off. That's the only part of the building that's actually alive.
-> hook
-- shift
KENNY: My last shift was eight months ago and I can still feel it. You ever test the same microtransaction flow four thousand times to make sure the BUY button never, ever fails? I have. The quit-to-desktop button fails all the time. The BUY button is flawless. That's not an accident. That's a priority.
-> hook
-- hook
ELLIOT (V.O.): He still has the badge. CRUNCH TEAM C. Nobody at Rockstarved ever deactivates anything; that would mean someone owning a task to completion.
* "I want to burn down the part of that building that's still alive. I need your badge." -> ask
* [SPEECH 35] "You could be the guy who ended Shark Cards. Give me the badge." -> ask_win | ask
* "Never mind." -> END
-- ask
KENNY: My badge. You're going up there to break Project Whale, aren't you. ...Take it. Take it, and when it's on the news, don't use my name, use my handle. kennyQA, all one word. I want the people who fired me to know a tester did it.
! give rs_badge 1 ; set kenny_badge ; quest mq_rs1 20 ; fame gamers 3 ; xp 50
-> END
-- ask_win
KENNY: The guy who ended Shark Cards. ...God. Yeah. Yes. Here. The badge still opens the service elevator; they never rotated the keys, rotating keys is a ticket and nobody closes tickets. Go. Make the confetti stop.
! give rs_badge 1 ; set kenny_badge ; set rs_floor_open ; quest mq_rs1 20 ; fame gamers 4 ; xp 60
-> END
-- lift
> He leans for his wallet and the lanyard swings loose. One motion. CRUNCH TEAM C, laminated, still warm from three years around his neck.
! give rs_badge 1 ; set kenny_badge ; quest mq_rs1 20 ; stab -1 ; xp 40
ELLIOT (V.O.): He'll notice it's gone tomorrow and assume he lost it, because losing things is what the job trained him to expect of himself.
-> END
-- lift_fail
KENNY: Hey — hey, that's mine. That's the only thing I kept. Don't. Please don't.
ELLIOT (V.O.): Too slow. I palm nothing and leave him his one souvenir. There's another way up.
-> END
-- after
? q.mq_ms.done -> ms_done
? q.mq_ms>=20 & !item.ms_invite>=1 -> ms
? !flag.rs_done -> waiting
KENNY: Did it work? ...It worked. My phone hasn't stopped. kennyQA is trending. People are sending me money. Strangers. For a badge. I'm going to cry into a better drink.
-> END
-- ms
> Kenny is turning a heavy embossed card over and over in his hands. MICROSLOP · FLOOR 101 · COMMUNITY VOICE.
KENNY: They want me in the front row. Clapping. While they announce they're closing eleven studios and replacing the testers with an AI that 'never needs a weekend.' They put a little confetti emoji in the email. Again with the confetti.
* "Give me the invitation. I'll clap for you." -> ms_give
* [SPEECH 40] "You don't have to be in the front row. You can be the reason there isn't one." -> ms_win | ms_give
* "Burn it. Don't go." -> ms_burn
-- ms_give
KENNY: You'll clap. Sure. You've got the face of a man who's about to clap REALLY hard on something. ...Take it. The chip in the corner calls the far-right elevator in the lobby. Floor 101.
! give ms_invite 1 ; quest mq_ms 30 ; xp 40
-> END
-- ms_win
KENNY: The reason there isn't one. ...Yeah. Take it. And hey: I tested their elevator firmware once, back at Rockstarved, freelance. The service hatch behind the stage isn't on any floor plan. It's where they keep the Library. You didn't hear that from kennyQA. You heard it from a tester who always reads the logs.
! give ms_invite 1 ; set ms_kenny_tip ; quest mq_ms 30 ; fame gamers 2 ; xp 60
-> END
-- ms_burn
KENNY: Burn it? That's... honestly the most tempting thing anyone's said to me since 'your severance is ready.' But no. You'd use it better. Here.
! give ms_invite 1 ; quest mq_ms 30 ; xp 40
-> END
-- ms_done
KENNY: A QA union. Me. Eleven thousand testers and a laminated badge over the bar. Every time I see a studio post a job with 'no crunch' in bold, I buy a round. I'm very poor now. It's great.
-> END
-- waiting
KENNY: You're still here? Floor 88. The service elevator in the WTC lobby, far left. Badge goes on the reader, reader goes beep, you go up. I've done it nine hundred times. Never once on a day I wanted to.
-> END

=== chi_cell
-- start
? flag.chi_cell_met -> again
> The warehouse is dark except for six monitors and a space heater. A young woman with a soldering iron waves without looking up. On the wall, a map of Chicago with two buildings circled in red.
TRENTON (TEXT): that's Ansel. Darlene vouches. he's good. - D
ANSEL: fsociety. The actual fsociety. Okay, playing it cool. Totally cool. Welcome to the Chicago cell, it's a warehouse, there's one chair, it's mine.
ANSEL: Two targets. Electronic Arse: the 'surprise mechanics' people. They call loot boxes surprise mechanics because 'gambling aimed at children' tested poorly with regulators. And Phony Interactive: they can revoke your whole library any time they want, and last year they did, to make a point about 'account security.'
ANSEL: Both of them run out of E Corp Midwest downtown. Same data floor. You hit one, you're already standing where you hit the other.
! set chi_cell_met ; quest mq_chi1 done ; quest mq_chi2 10 ; fame gamers 3 ; xp 80
* "Start with the loot boxes." -> boxes
* "Why do you fight for this?" -> why
-- boxes
ANSEL: The loot boxes. Yeah. Get onto the Midwest data floor and rig Electronic Arse's drop server. I don't want to delete it. I want to make it HONEST. Print the real odds on the box. Watch the whole thing fall apart when people can see the one-in-nine-thousand.
-> END
-- why
ANSEL: My little brother. He's eleven. He asked me if he was 'bad at the game' or if the game was 'built to make him feel bad so he'd buy the thing.' Eleven years old, and he figured out the business model from the inside of the sadness. I'd burn the world down for that kid. Starting with this building.
! trust robot 1
-> boxes
-- again
? flag.phony_freed -> done
? flag.earse_exposed -> half
ANSEL: Data floor's at the back of E Corp Midwest. Loot-box server, then the account authority. One trip if you're fast and nobody sounds the alarm.
* "How do I get past the lobby?" -> lobby
* "See you." -> END
-- lobby
ANSEL: Daytime, it's just a lobby, walk in like you're late for a meeting. Night, they lock it down: you'll want an E Corp uniform. Every one of these towers buys the same security package. If you got into the one in New York, you know this one already.
-> END
-- half
ANSEL: My brother texted me a screenshot. The real odds, right there on the box, 1 in 9,214. He wrote 'lol no.' Two words. Best review that company ever got. Now go flip Phony. Same terminal.
-> END
-- done
? flag.ansel_bye -> done_again
ANSEL: You did both. Both! Okay. I'm not crying, the space heater makes my eyes water. It does. It's a known issue.
ANSEL: Go home, fsociety. Tell Darlene the Chicago cell says thanks. And if you ever need a mod chip, a drone, or a very angry eleven-year-old's opinion on game design, you know where the warehouse is.
* "Look after your brother." -> bye_bro
* "Keep the cell running. They'll come back." -> bye_cell
-- bye_bro
ANSEL: Always. He wants to be a game designer now. An HONEST one. I told him that's the most punk thing a person can be.
! set ansel_bye ; trust robot 1 ; stab 2 ; xp 30
-> END
-- bye_cell
ANSEL: They always come back. That's what live service means: it never ends, and neither do we.
! set ansel_bye ; give usb_drive 2 ; xp 30
-> END
-- done_again
ANSEL: Still here. Still one chair. Still mine.
-> END

=== brad
-- start
? flag.ms_done -> after
? flag.ms_pw -> again
> A man in a black blazer over a T-shirt that costs more than the blazer, practicing a laugh in a reflective screen. CHIEF GAMING OFFICER, says the lanyard. He clocks your invitation and his smile switches on like a sign.
BRAD: kennyQA! Or... kennyQA's plus-one? Love it. Love the energy. You're going to be SO excited at about nine-oh-four. That's when I say 'one more thing.'
* "What's the one more thing?" -> thing
* "You're closing the studios today." -> studios
* "Nice to meet you." -> END
-- thing
BRAD: Can't spoil it! But imagine: every game you've ever loved, forever, for one low monthly price. And imagine never having to worry about owning anything again. Ownership is so stressful. We're removing the stress. We're removing the friction. We're removing...
BRAD: ...anyway. Pastries are by the elevator.
-> hook
-- studios
BRAD: 'Closing' is such a negative word. We're SUNSETTING. The studios' legacies live on, through subscription access and our generative partner, SlopForge, which honours their style at a fraction of the... honour.
ELLIOT (V.O.): He believes it. That's the worst part. He's the kind of man who believes whatever is on the slide in front of him, and he's never once been in the room where the slides get written.
-> hook
-- hook
ELLIOT (V.O.): The Library's behind the stage. He signed the launch switch. He'll have the password somewhere he can reach it quickly. Men like Brad never make anything hard for Brad.
* [SPEECH 55] "Brad, I'm from the board. Security audit before the switch. I need the Library credentials, now, quietly." -> pw | pw_fail
* [HACKING 45] (His phone's on the stage, unlocked, mid-rehearsal. Glance at the password manager while he practices his laugh.) -> peek | peek_fail
* "Enjoy your keynote, Brad." -> END
-- pw
BRAD: The board? Oh god. Oh, okay, yes. Totally. Quietly. It's 'GamePassAway!' Capital G, capital P, capital A, exclamation point, I'm very security-minded. Please tell them I was helpful. Please tell them I'm a culture fit.
! set ms_pw ; xp 60
-> END
-- pw_fail
BRAD: The board would have emailed. The board always emails. With, like, a calendar invite. ...Security? No, you know what, I don't want to make a thing. Enjoy the pastries. Far, far from the stage.
ELLIOT (V.O.): He'll keep an eye on me now. The Library will have to be done the hard way.
-> END
-- peek
> He turns to the screen to rehearse. His phone lies face-up beside the podium, the password manager open to a single entry: LIBRARY MASTER. You read it twice and look away before he turns back.
ELLIOT (V.O.): 'GamePassAway!' Of course it is.
! set ms_pw ; xp 50
-> END
-- peek_fail
> You lean toward the phone. Brad turns, and the phone goes into his blazer with a practiced hand. He winks at you like you're both in on something.
BRAD: Ah-ah. No spoilers.
-> END
-- again
BRAD: Two minutes to keynote! Find your seat! Actually, stand. Standing reads as excitement on camera.
-> END
-- after
BRAD: Do you know what happened? Nobody knows what happened. My badge doesn't work. My phone keeps showing me a little line of text that says YOU OWN WHAT YOU PAID FOR and I don't know how to make it stop.
-> END
